+# Tracing profilers
+
+A tracing profiler (`Nsight Systems`, the profilers of `ROCm`, ...) shows
+on a time line the ranges that a program declares through an annotation
+library (`NVTX`, `roctx`, ...). The function `setProfilingMarkers` sets
+two functions, called at the beginning of each active profiling section,
+with its name, and at its end:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+static void beginRange(const char* const n) noexcept { nvtxRangePushA(n); }
+static void endRange() noexcept { nvtxRangePop(); }
+
+mgis::setProfilingMarkers({&beginRange, &endRange});
+~~~~
+
+The sections are then seen in the trace, with their names and at each of
+their calls. The range of a section is the interval measured by its
+timer: it begins after the synchronization of the beginning of the
+section and ends after the one of its end (see above), so that it
+contains the computations launched asynchronously by the section. The
+functions are shared by all contexts. By default, none is set, and a
+section then only tests a pointer. `setProfilingMarkers` returns the
+previous functions, and `getProfilingMarkers` the current ones. A section
+which began before the call is not reported.
+
+A `ProfilingRange` object declares a range which is only reported by
+these functions: it is neither timed nor added to the profiling tree. It
+is meant for the parts of a computation which are worth seeing in a
+trace under a name changing at each call, such as the time steps or the
+iterations of an algorithm, which would add one node per call to the
+profiling tree:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+for (int i = 0; i != n; ++i) {
+  // the name is only built if the range is reported
+  mgis::ProfilingRange r(ctx, [i] { return "step " + std::to_string(i); });
+  CatchTimeSection(ctx, "Solve");
+  // ...
+}
+~~~~
+
+A range does nothing if no function is set or if the profiling of the
+context is disabled. Otherwise, it also waits for the computations
+launched asynchronously, at its beginning and at its end. Ranges and
+sections must be properly nested.
+
 # Aggregation and Loops
 
 To prevent the profiling tree from growing indefinitely and consuming too much memory, the system automatically aggregates repeated calls to the same section within the same parent scope. 
